ile-injector, connection-notebook | kubeflow/notebooks | notebooks (kubeflow.org) | mutating | Fail |\n203\t\n204\t### Cross-Cutting Concerns\n205\t\n206\t| Concern | Components | Affected Resources | Pattern |\n207\t|---------|------------|--------------------|---------|\n208\t| InferenceService lifecycle | kserve, odh-model-controller | inferenceservices.serving.kserve.io | Expected layering: kserve validates/defaults core CRD semantics; odh-model-controller adds platform-specific mutations (auth, monitoring, networking) |\n209\t| Hardware profile injection | rhods-operator, workbenches-operator | hardwareprofiles (infrastructure.opendatahub.io), notebooks (kubeflow.org) | Expected layering: rhods-operator validates HardwareProfile CRDs; workbenches-operator injects resolved profiles into notebook pods |\n210\t| Pod mutation for serving | kserve | pods | KServe injects sidecar containers into inference pods for storage initialization and model agent |\n211\t| Monitoring injection | rhods-operator | podmonitors (monitoring.coreos.com) | Platform operator injects monitoring labels into PodMonitor resources |\n212\t\n213\t### Overlay Deployment\n214\t\n215\tOverlay membership data is not available for this generation. Webhook entries reflect manifest-level inventory only.\n216\t\n217\t## Data Flows\n218\t\n219\t### Key Platform Workflows\n220\t\n221\t#### Workflow 1: Model Deployment via Dashboard\n222\t\n223\t| Step | Component | Action | Next Component |\n224\t|------|-----------|--------|----------------|\n225\t| 1 | odh-dashboard | User creates InferenceService/LLMInferenceService via UI; BFF sidecar validates via SubjectAccessReview | kube-apiserver |\n226\t| 2 | kube-apiserver | Admission webhooks validate and default the CRD resource | kserve, odh-model-controller |\n227\t| 3 | kserve (manager or llmisvc) | Reconciles InferenceService: creates Deployment, Service, HTTPRoute/Route, HPA/KEDA ScaledObject | Kubernetes API |\n228\t| 4 | odh-model-controller | Reconciles InferenceService: adds NetworkPolicy, ServiceMonitor, PodMonitor, Route, Kuadrant AuthPolicy | Kubernetes API |\n229\t| 5 | Gateway controller | HTTPRoute attaches to data-science-gateway; external traffic routed through Envoy to model pod | Model server pod |\n230\t\n231\t#### Workflow 2: Models-as-a-Service API Request\n232\t\n233\t| Step | Component | Action | Next Component |\n234\t|------|-----------|--------|----------------|\n235\t| 1 | External client | Sends request with API key/token to maas-default-gateway | Gateway (Envoy) |\n236\t| 2 | Kuadrant/Authorino | AuthPolicy validates: API key callback to maas-api, TokenReview, optional OIDC JWT; OPA authorizes subscription/group | maas-api |\n237\t| 3 | maas-api | Processes model listing, subscription management, or API key lifecycle; queries PostgreSQL for tenant state | PostgreSQL |\n238\t| 4 | maas-controller | MaaSModelRefReconciler watches KServe LLMInferenceService for model availability; creates HTTPRoutes for model endpoints | kserve |\n239\t\n240\t#### Workflow 3: Notebook Workspace Creation\n241\t\n242\t| Step | Component | Action | Next Component |\n243\t|------|-----------|--------|----------------|\n244\t| 1 | odh-dashboard | User creates Notebook resource via notebooks UI module | kube-apiserver |\n245\t| 2 | workbenches-operator | Mutating webhook injects HardwareProfile resources and data-connection secrets into notebook pod template | kube-apiserver |\n246\t| 3 | kubeflow (notebook-controller) | Reconciles Notebook resource: creates StatefulSet, Service, PodDisruptionBudget | Kubernetes API |\n247\t| 4 | rhods-operator | Gateway controller creates Route for notebook access via kube-auth-proxy OIDC authentication | OpenShift router |\n248\t\n249\t#### Workflow 4: Pipeline Execution\n250\t\n251\t| Step | Component | Action | Next Component |\n252\t|------|-----------|--------|----------------|\n253\t| 1 | odh-dashboard | User submits pipeline run via pipelines UI module | data-science-pipelines API server |\n254\t| 2 | data-science-pipelines-operator | Manages DSPA resources: deploys API server, persistence agent, scheduled workflow controller | Kubernetes API |\n255\t| 3 | data-science-pipelines | API server creates Argo Workflow; scheduled workflow controller manages recurring runs | argo-workflows |\n256\t| 4 | argo-workflows | Executes workflow steps as pods; artifact storage via SeaweedFS/S3 | Pipeline component pods |\n257\t\n258\t#### Workflow 5: LLM Disaggregated Inference (llm-d)\n259\t\n260\t| Step | Component | Action | Next Component |\n261\t|------|-----------|--------|----------------|\n262\t| 1 | kserve (llmisvc) | Reconciles LLMInferenceService: creates prefill and decode Deployments, InferencePool, Gateway resources | Kubernetes API |\n263\t| 2 | llm-d-router (EPP) | Endpoint Picker Plugin selects backend pods through Gateway API InferencePool; model-aware routing via Envoy sidecar | llm-d decode/prefill pods |\n264\t| 3 | llm-d (decode pods) | vLLM OpenAI-compatible API server serves inference requests; KV cache coordination via ModelExpress gRPC | llm-d-kv-cache |\n265\t| 4 | workload-variant-autoscaler | Monitors InferencePool metrics via Prometheus; scales decode/prefill replicas via KEDA | Kubernetes API |\n266\t\n267\t## Deployment Architecture\n268\t\n269\t### Deployment Topology\n270\t\n271\tThe platform deploys across four namespace tiers. The operator namespace (redhat-ods-operator or opendatahub-operator-system) hosts rhods-operator and the platform-level admission webhooks. The applications namespace (redhat-ods-applications or opendatahub) hosts the dashboard, model controllers, and module operators. Per-component namespaces (kserve, models-as-a-service) isolate specific subsystems. User namespaces host workloads: inference service pods, notebook StatefulSets, pipeline workflow pods, training jobs, and Ray clusters.\n272\t\n273\tThe operator tier manages the control plane: rhods-operator reconciles DataScienceCluster and DSCInitialization CRs, triggering per-component operators to deploy their workloads. Module operators (kserve-module, ai-gateway-operator, trainer-operator, workbenches-operator, etc.) each run as separate processes with dedicated RBAC identities and leader election, following the ODH module operator pattern for standardized manifest rendering and lifecycle management.\n274\t\n275\tGateway infrastructure is deployed in the openshift-ingress namespace, where the data-science-gateway receives HTTPRoutes from multiple components. Monitoring infrastructure (Prometheus collectors and proxies with kube-rbac-proxy sidecars) is deployed in the applications namespace by the rhods-operator Monitoring service controller.\n276\t\n277\t### Resource Summary\n278\t\n279\tThe platform comprises 72 analyzed components including: 19+ operators/controllers (rhods-operator, kserve x4, odh-model-controller, odh-dashboard-operator, models-as-a-service, model-registry-operator, ai-gateway-operator, trainer-operator, workbenches-operator, feast-module-operator, mlflow-operator, data-science-pipelines-operator, kuberay, kueue-operator, spark-operator, training-operator, trustyai-service-operator, ogx-k8s-operator, mcp-lifecycle-operator, mcp-lifecycle-module-operator, llm-d-batch-gateway-operator), 145 CRDs, 210 admission webhooks, 122 services, and 1658 RBAC entries across all components.\n280\t\n281\t### Deployment Manifest Patterns\n282\t\n283\t#### Kustomize Patterns\n284\t\n285\t| Pattern | Components Using | Description |\n286\t|---------|------------------|-------------|\n287\t| Distribution overlays (odh/rhoai) | kserve, odh-model-controller, odh-dashboard, rhods-operator, model-registry-operator | Separate kustomize overlays for ODH and RHOAI distributions with image replacements, patch additions, and RBAC modifications |\n288\t| Template-based rendering | rhods-operator, model-registry-operator | Go template manifests rendered at reconciliation time with runtime values (namespace, image references, configuration) |\n289\t| Manifest bundling in container image | trainer-operator, workbenches-operator | Manifests copied into operator container at build time from /opt/manifests-template/ directories |\n290\t| Server-side apply with field ownership | trainer-operator, workbenches-operator | Framework deploy action applies resources with component-specific field manager names, enabling precise garbage collection |\n291\t\n292\t#### Distribution Variants\n293\t\n294\t| Component | ODH Path | RHOAI Path | Key Differences |\n295\t|-----------|----------|------------|-----------------|\n296\t| kserve | config/overlays/odh/ | config/overlays/rhoai/ (downstream builds) | ODH removes upstream kube-rbac-proxy sidecar, replaces with odh-kube-auth-proxy; RHOAI adds FIPS build flags |\n297\t| odh-dashboard | manifests/odh/ | manifests/rhoai/ | Federation configuration differs (module enablement); RHOAI adds additional BFF sidecars |\n298\t| rhods-operator | config/overlays/odh/ | config/overlays/rhoai/ | Component manifests differ; RHOAI includes additional component CRDs |\n299\t\n300\t#### Manifest Consumption Pipeline\n301\t\n302\tComponent manifests flow from upstream open-source repositories through midstream integration branches (opendatahub-io organization) to downstream distribution branches (red-hat-data-services organization). The auto_merge sync mechanism maintains branch alignment. rhods-operator's get_all_manifests.sh script (or equivalent pipeline tooling) aggregates component manifests into the operator repository, where they are rendered and deployed to the cluster during reconciliation. Konflux build pipelines produce container images with FIPS-compliant build flags and UBI base images.\n303\t\n304\t### High Availability\n305\t\n306\t| Component | Replicas | HA Mechanism |\n307\t|-----------|----------|--------------|\n308\t| rhods-operator | 1 | Leader election via controller-runtime |\n309\t| kserve-controller-manager | 1 | Leader election via ConfigMap/Lease |\n310\t| kserve llmisvc controller | 1 | Leader election via Lease |\n311\t| kserve-module controller | 1 | Leader election via Lease |\n312\t| odh-model-controller | 1 | Leader election via controller-runtime |\n313\t| odh-dashboard | Configurable | Stateless replicas; session state in Redis/Valkey for kube-auth-proxy multi-replica |\n314\t| models-as-a-service (maas-api) | Configurable | Stateless API server; state in PostgreSQL |\n315\t| kueue-operator | 1 | Leader election |\n316\t| Model serving pods | Configurable | HPA, KEDA ScaledObjects, LeaderWorkerSets for multi-node inference |\n317\t\n318\t### Disconnected Support\n319\t\n320\tDisconnected deployment support is unresolved across all 66 components that lack explicit disconnected_deployment evidence. The platform is expected to support air-gapped deployments through the OLM `RELATED_IMAGE` pattern and oc-mirror for image mirroring, but no complete deterministic evidence family was extracted from the analyzed component sources for this generation. Components that reference container images by digest (via Konflux build pipelines) are compatible with mirrored registries, but the specific image-override and proxy configuration mechanisms were not confirmed from the structured inventory.\n321\t\n322\t### Multi-Architecture Support\n323\t\n324\t| Architecture | Support Level | Notes |\n325\t|--------------|---------------|-------|\n326\t| amd64 | Full | Primary build and test architecture for all components |\n327\t| arm64 | Partial | Some upstream components support arm64; RHOAI distribution support unresolved |\n328\t| ppc64le | Unresolved | No evidence of ppc64le builds in analyzed components |\n329\t| s390x | Unresolved | No evidence of s390x builds in analyzed components |\n330\t\n331\t## Platform Maturity\n332\t\n333\t- **Total Components**: 72\n334\t- **Operator-based Components**: 19+\n335\t- **Gateway API Coverage**: Primary ingress mechanism; dual-path with OpenShift Routes for backward compatibility\n336\t- **mTLS Enforcement**: Conditional (STRICT when Istio/SAIL present for llm-d control plane; platform-wide mTLS depends on Istio deployment)\n337\t- **CRD API Versions**: v1alpha1 (majority), v1alpha2 (LLMInferenceService), v1beta1 (InferenceService), v1 (DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, HardwareProfile), v2 (DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization)\n338\t- **FIPS Compliance**: All shipped Go binaries built with strictfipsruntime; Python components inherit FIPS from UBI base OpenSSL\n339\t- **Disconnected Support**: Unresolved (expected via OLM RELATED_IMAGE pattern but not confirmed from source)\n340\t- **Multi-Architecture**: amd64 (full); arm64, ppc64le, s390x (unresolved)\n341\t\n342\t## Platform Architectural Analysis\n343\t\n344\tRHOAI 3.6-ea.1 represents a platform in active architectural evolution across several dimensions. The most significant patterns and tensions observed across all 72 component analyses are:\n345\t\n346\t**Module operator proliferation.** The platform has adopted a module operator pattern (via odh-platform-utilities framework) where each major capability runs its own operator process with independent RBAC, leader election, and lifecycle management. This provides clean separation of concerns and independent release cadences but results in a large number of operator pods (19+ controllers), each with substantial RBAC footprints. The ai-gateway-operator alone has 128 RBAC entries spanning 10+ API groups. This proliferation creates operational complexity in monitoring, debugging, and resource consumption.\n347\t\n348\t**Authentication fragmentation.** The platform uses at least four distinct authentication patterns: (1) kube-auth-proxy OIDC at the gateway level for dashboard access, (2) Kuadrant/Authorino AuthPolicy for MaaS API access with API keys, TokenReview, and OIDC, (3) kube-rbac-proxy sidecars for pod-level SubjectAccessReview enforcement on metrics and API endpoints, and (4) application-level TokenReview/SubjectAccessReview in component code. While each pattern serves a valid use case, the lack of a unified authentication architecture means that different capabilities present different credential requirements and failure modes to users and operators.\n349\t\n350\t**Gateway API transition.** The platform is mid-migration from OpenShift Routes to Gateway API. The rhods-operator provisions a central data-science-gateway, but not all components have migrated: model-registry-operator maintains dual ingress, some components still use Routes exclusively, and models-as-a-service references a separately-named gateway (maas-default-gateway). The conditional Istio integration (EnvoyFilter/DestinationRule created only when Istio CRDs are detected) adds complexity to the ingress story, as the authentication chain differs between Istio-present and Istio-absent clusters.\n351\t\n352\t**KServe multi-controller complexity.** KServe ships 4 independent controller binaries (manager, llmisvc, localmodel/localmodelnode) plus the kserve-module operator, totaling 5 processes with separate RBAC identities. The llmisvc controller alone manages 13 resource types including HPA, KEDA ScaledObjects, LeaderWorkerSets, InferencePools, and Gateway resources. This reflects the rapid feature expansion of the LLM serving stack (disaggregated inference, multi-node serving, local model caching) but creates a large blast radius for any KServe upgrade.\n353\t\n354\t**Shared infrastructure dependencies.** Multiple components independently depend on cert-manager, prometheus-operator, and Gateway API, each implementing their own conditional watch patterns. This means the platform has soft dependencies on these infrastructure components without a centralized dependency management mechanism — each component independently checks for CRD availability and degrades independently.\n355\t\n356\t**Supply chain consistency.** All analyzed Go components use the FIPS-compliant build pattern (CGO_ENABLED=1 + GOEXPERIMENT=strictfipsruntime) on UBI 9 base images, providing strong build-time FIPS posture. However, the upstream KServe core manager Dockerfile uses CGO_ENABLED=0 (static linking, not FIPS-compliant), relying on the Konflux build pipeline to override these flags for downstream builds. Python components (KServe model servers, data-science-pipelines) inherit FIPS from the UBI base OpenSSL but have no explicit FIPS opt-in in their application code.\n357\t\n358\t**Unresolved cross-cutting topics.** High availability, disconnected deployment, and supply chain evidence remain unresolved across the majority of components. While the platform architecture supports HA through leader election (operators) and horizontal scaling (workloads), no deterministic evidence of replica configuration, failover behavior, or disruption budgets was extracted at the platform level. Disconnected deployment support is architecturally expected through OLM and oc-mirror but was not confirmed from component source analysis.\n359\t\n360\t### Serving Path Evolution\n361\t\n362\t| Path | Evidence Components | Entry/Routing Surface | Runtime or Provider Role | Source |\n363\t|------|---------------------|-----------------------|--------------------------|--------|\n364\t| KServe InferenceService (single-model) | kserve (manager), odh-model-controller | Gateway API HTTPRoute or OpenShift Route -> model server pod | vLLM, OpenVINO, MLServer, HuggingFace, sklearn, XGBoost, LightGBM, TorchServe, Triton model servers via ClusterServingRuntime | kserve config/runtimes/, pkg/controller/v1beta1/inferenceservice/controller.go |\n365\t| KServe LLMInferenceService (llm-d disaggregated) | kserve (llmisvc), llm-d, llm-d-router, llm-d-kv-cache, llm-d-routing-sidecar, workload-variant-autoscaler | Gateway API -> InferencePool -> EPP (llm-d-router) -> Envoy sidecar -> prefill/decode pods | vLLM with disaggregated prefill/decode; ModelExpress KV cache broker; KEDA/WVA autoscaling | kserve pkg/controller/v1alpha2/llmisvc/controller.go, llm-d guides/recipes/ |\n366\t| KServe InferenceGraph (DAG routing) | kserve (manager, router) | Gateway API or Route -> kserve router binary | Router authenticates via K8s TokenReview/SubjectAccessReview, fans out to model nodes per graph topology | kserve cmd/router/main.go |\n367\t| Models-as-a-Service (multi-tenant gateway) | models-as-a-service, kserve (via LLMInferenceService watch) | maas-default-gateway HTTPRoute -> Kuadrant AuthPolicy -> maas-api | OpenAI-compatible REST API; subscription governance; delegates to KServe LLMInferenceService backends | models-as-a-service deployment/base/maas-api/networking/httproute.yaml |\n368\t| Model Registry serving | model-registry, model-registry-operator | Gateway API HTTPRoute (data-science-gateway) or OpenShift Route -> kube-rbac-proxy -> model-registry REST API | Model metadata storage and retrieval; PostgreSQL backend | model-registry-operator internal/controller/config/templates/ |\n369\t\n370\t## Version-Specific Changes (3.6-ea.1)\n371\t\n372\t| Component | Changes |\n373\t|-----------|---------|\n374\t| rhods-operator | Active development: latest component image syncs (odh-dashboard, cluster-validator); auto-merge from upstream opendatahub-operator |\n375\t| kserve | Upstream main merge; Konflux pipeline sync; gatekeeper workflow additions |\n376\t| models-as-a-service | Upstream main merge; gatekeeper workflow; UBI toolset digest updates |\n377\t| kube-auth-proxy | Upstream main merge; gatekeeper workflow; UBI toolset digest updates |\n378\t| odh-model-controller | Upstream main merge; gatekeeper workflow additions |\n379\t| ai-gateway-operator | Upstream stable merge |\n380\t| model-registry-operator | Upstream stable merge |\n381\t| workbenches-operator | Upstream v1.x merge |\n382\t| trainer-operator | Upstream main merge |\n383\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-abfbe2b1297d6a9a","agent_id":"aad452a5752ce3ae8","session_id":"ffc190d1-a457-4608-ad90-0fad47057ac1","uuid":"dde0f343-7