component teams**: Switch final runtime stage to PQC base image by September 15, or file OCP exception\" — this is listed under Impacted Teams, i.e., the strategy's primary HLR. If the decomposition produced no switch epics, the review agent would flag \"HLR-1 not covered by any epic — no orphaned requirements violated\". The HLR traceability matrix needs HLR→epic mapping. HLR-1 must map to epics that actually deliver PQC base images.\n\n   So switch epics are needed. Grouping: I'll use the strategy's base-image families, and assign ownership to the team that the strategy/architecture suggests coordinates that family. Actually — you know what, let me look at the families again with team lens:\n\n   - **ubi9-minimal family (~68 images, \"Go operators, most platform components\")**: These are Go components. Per the strategy assumption, \"Go components get ML-KEM for free via native Go crypto; while the base image switch is not functionally required for ML-KEM, it should be done for platform consistency\". So for ~68 images, the switch is a mechanical Dockerfile change + rebuild. Multiple teams own these images. The coordination is DevOps/Build (\"Coordinate base image pipeline updates\"). \n   \n     Option: One umbrella epic \"Switch ubi9-minimal-based images to ubi9/ubi-minimal-pqc\" owned by DevOps/Build with AC that each component team's linked ticket completes their switch. Hmm — but rule 1 says different team → separate epic...\n   \n     Alternatively: don't create a switch epic for this family at all; instead the coordination epic's AC covers \"all linked team tickets for ubi9-minimal images completed or exception filed\". \n\n   I keep going back and forth. Let me look at how the pipeline/review works — is there an example decomposition from synthetic cases I can calibrate against? The workspace only has this case's files. Let me check .claude-config or docs/ for the epic-decompose skill docs — the skill directory might have more context (criteria, review pro