{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-c74fedb5-15a8-44c3-97a6-602694c4f086","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8acfd2e76feb007f","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic_case-021-real-pqc-base-image-compliance_0_yhs98llf/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8001-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8001 (Base image compliance — Post-quantum cryptography support)\n\n## Triage Decision\n\nFull decomposition (not below-threshold, not docs-only). The strategy is S-sized, but it affects all RHOAI component images (~153 relatedImages across every component team), names three distinct impacted teams, and contains genuine unknowns whose answers change which downstream epics exist (PQC variants of python/nodejs base images, AIPCC RHEL 9.8 rebase date, Rust TLS postures). The below-threshold escape conditions for genuine unknowns and multiple architecturally distinct work streams both apply.\n\n## Decomposition Approach\n\nThe strategy is a compliance coordination tracker: each component team owns switching its images or filing an OCP exception, and links its tracking tickets to the parent strategy for aggregated visibility. The epic set therefore follows the strategy's own decomposition axes:\n\n- **Base-image-family switch campaigns** — one epic per row of the strategy's base image mapping table. Each campaign is a single logical change (final runtime stage swap to one named target base) executed per owning team via linked tracking tickets; the campaign epic is coordinated by the DevOps/Build team, whose stated involvement is coordinating base image pipeline updates.\n- **Named component remediations** — fms-guardrails-orchestrator (rustls+ring) and trainer data cache (Rust TLS posture unknown) each get an Investigation (the strategy's named spikes) plus a gated remediation Implementation.\n- **Central work streams** — AIPCC base image variant confirmation (Investigation), compliance coordination/readiness matrix, automated payload CI verification scan, and performance validation.\n\n**Component mapping notes (non-obvious mappings per Step 2):** the canonical RHAI component list has no entry for a platform-wide image campaign, so each family campaign maps to the closest parent-level canonical component: ubi9-minimal family → `AI Core Platform`; rhel9-6-els family (notebooks, workbenches, training runtimes, mlserver) → `Notebooks Images` (family also spans `Model Runtimes` and `Workbenches/IDE` images, recorded in epic scope); python/nodejs family (training variants, lmes-job, feature-server, dashboard) → `Model Runtimes` (family also spans `Feature Store` and `AI Core Dashboard` images, recorded in epic scope). fms-guardrails-orchestrator → `AI Guardrails`; trainer data cache → `Training Kubeflow` (trainer component); AIPCC base image work → `AIPCC Productization`; coordination → `DevOps`; CI scan → `CI/CD`; performance validation → `PerfScale`.\n\n**Conditional decomposition decision:** the strategy's bounded unknowns (PQC variant availability; RHEL 9.8 rebase date; Rust TLS postures) each have two outcomes, but the \"negative\" outcomes (teams file OCP exceptions instead of switching / no remediation needed) are explicitly out of scope — each team files its own exception. Negative outcomes therefore produce no branch epics. The gated-epic mechanism (`gated_by` + `gate_failure_impact` with `remove`/`rewrite` actions) is used instead of `-BRANCH-` files, and the exception path is tracked by the compliance coordination epic's acceptance criteria.\n\n**External dependency modeling (Rule 21):** AIPCC's delivery of PQC python/nodejs base image variants is a third-party deliverable from RHOAI's perspective, tracked by RHOAIENG-7195/AIPCC-7258. It is modeled as a precondition with tiered delivery (see Tiered Delivery) — an Investigation epic to confirm availability and committed dates (owned by the impacted AIPCC team), not an RHOAI implementation epic that builds the images.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| E001 | Confirm PQC base image variant availability and RHEL 9.8 rebase date with AIPCC | Investigation | AIPCC team | P0 |\n| E002 | Switch ubi9-minimal-based runtime images to ubi9/ubi-minimal-pqc | Implementation | DevOps/Build | P0 |\n| E003 | Switch rhel9-ELS-based runtime images to rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal | Implementation | DevOps/Build | P0 |\n| E004 | Switch python/nodejs-based runtime images to PQC base image variants | Implementation | DevOps/Build | P0 |\n| E005 | Spike: fms-guardrails-orchestrator TLS migration path to ML-KEM hybrid key exchange | Investigation | AI Guardrails team | P0 |\n| E006 | Migrate fms-guardrails-orchestrator TLS stack to ML-KEM hybrid key exchange | Implementation | AI Guardrails team | P0 |\n| E007 | Audit trainer data cache Rust TLS posture | Investigation | Trainer team | P0 |\n| E008 | Remediate trainer data cache TLS stack for ML-KEM hybrid support | Implementation | Trainer team | P0 |\n| E009 | PQC readiness matrix and compliance coordination | Implementation | DevOps/Build | P0 |\n| E010 | Automated payload CI scan for PQC crypto policy compliance | Implementation | DevOps/Build | P0 |\n| E011 | TLS handshake latency regression threshold and measurement | Implementation | Performance team | P0 |\n\nAll epics are P0 because every non-deferred HLR in the strategy is P0 (hard September 15, 2026 compliance deadline), and the performance validation epic is required before ship per the strategy's Performance Team Support prerequisite.\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 AIPCC variant/rebase confirmation (Investigation)\"]\n    E002[\"E002 ubi9-minimal family switch campaign\"]\n    E003[\"E003 rhel9-ELS family switch campaign\"]\n    E004[\"E004 python/nodejs family switch campaign\"]\n    E005[\"E005 guardrails TLS migration spike (Investigation)\"]\n    E006[\"E006 guardrails TLS stack migration\"]\n    E007[\"E007 trainer data cache TLS audit (Investigation)\"]\n    E008[\"E008 trainer data cache TLS remediation\"]\n    E009[\"E009 readiness matrix and compliance coordination\"]\n    E010[\"E010 payload CI crypto-policy scan\"]\n    E011[\"E011 TLS handshake regression threshold and measurement\"]\n\n    E001 --> E003\n    E001 --> E004\n    E005 --> E006\n    E007 --> E008\n```\n\nRoots: E001, E002, E005, E007, E009, E010, E011 (all start immediately). Critical path length: 2.\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream Implementation) | The AIPCC RHEL 9.8 rebase committed date (AIPCC-7258) gates RHEL9-ELS image adoption per the strategy's Dependencies table and Open Questions. If the rebase date slips past the deadline, the ~34 rhel9-6-els-based teams file exceptions instead — the campaign's scope changes. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream Implementation) | PQC variants of ubi9/python-311, python-312, python-312-minimal, nodejs-22 are unconfirmed (RHOAIENG-7195). If not available in time, the switch epic does not exist — affected teams file OCP exceptions. True gate: `remove`. |\n| E005 → E006 | Rules 3, 12 (spike determines migration approach; migration builds on the spike's chosen path) | The rustls+ring → ML-KEM-capable stack migration approach is unresolved (spike RHOAIENG-7452). The migration epic's approach is rewritten by the spike outcome. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n| E007 → E008 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream Implementation) | The trainer data cache TLS audit (RHOAIENG-7196) determines whether remediation work exists at all: a pure-Rust TLS stack requires migration per the Security NFR; an OpenSSL-backed stack inherits PQC from the base image switch and no remediation epic is needed. True gate: `remove`. |\n| No edge E001 → E002 | Rule 14 | `ubi9/ubi-minimal-pqc` is confirmed available (Tobin Pellicort, August 2026; OCPSTRAT-7413). The ~68-image ubi9-minimal campaign has no unresolved dependency and starts immediately. |\n| No edge E002 ↔ E006, E002 ↔ E008 | Rules 13, 14 | The guardrails/trainer TLS stack migrations are independent of the base image swap — rustls+ring does not use system OpenSSL, so the base switch does not deliver PQC to those components and the TLS migration does not depend on it. Different work surfaces, parallel. |\n| No edge campaigns → E010 | Rule 13 (configuration reference, not build-against artifact) | The CI scan consumes built payload images at verification time — a configuration reference (image digests), enforced as an acceptance criterion (final scan across the RHOAI 3.6 payload passes once switches land), not a DAG dependency. Wiring an edge would serialize scan development behind every team's switch. |\n| No edge campaigns → E011 | Rule 13 | The performance measurement runs against built images at verification time. Threshold definition and methodology (referencing PSASTRAT-7170) start immediately; the final payload measurement is an AC-gated end state, not a build-against dependency. |\n| No edge E009 ↔ any campaign | Rules 14, 15 | The readiness matrix and coordination run in parallel with the campaigns; matrix entries for python/nodejs targets are authored as TBD and updated when the AIPCC confirmation resolves. Coordination does not consume any epic's build artifact. |\n| No edge E010 ↔ E011 | Rule 14 | Different work products (CI compliance scan vs. performance benchmark) with no shared artifacts. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epics |\n|-----|----------|-------|\n| All RHOAI runtime images must use a PQC-enabled base image (`ubi9-minimal-pqc`, `rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal`, or equivalent) for RHOAI 3.6 / OCP 5.0 | P0 | E002, E003, E004 (switch delivery); E001 (gating investigation); E006, E008 (non-OpenSSL components' route to ML-KEM compliance); E010 (verification) |\n| Teams unable to comply by September 15 must file an OCP exception via OCPEXCEPT with a remediation plan | P0 | E009 (exception process enforcement, tracking, zero-unaccounted-images accounting); E006, E008 (fallback paths recorded in gate_failure_impact) |\n| PQC enablement is runtime only — only the final stage of the Dockerfile/Containerfile changes; no builder image change needed | P0 | E002, E003, E004 (scope constraint and acceptance criterion on each campaign) |\n| PQC-signed X.509 certificates (ML-DSA) | Deferred | Explicitly out of scope per strategy — no epic, deferred to follow-on phase |\n\nStrategy acceptance criteria allocation: AC-1 (DEFAULT:PQ + ML-KEM kem-algorithms, measured by automated CI scan) → E010 delivers the scan; E002/E003/E004 carry the per-family pass criteria. AC-2 (every image switched or exceptioned by September 15, zero unaccounted) → E009 plus the campaigns. AC-3 (team tracking tickets linked to the parent strategy) → E009.\n\n## Health Warnings (non-blocking)\n\n1. **Performance NFR has no numeric threshold** — flagged NEEDS DOMAIN INPUT, owner PM + Performance team lead, \"must be resolved before Sprint 2 ends\". Modeled as an explicit deliverable and acceptance criterion on E011 rather than a separate Investigation (the threshold value does not change which epics exist; measurement proceeds either way). Requires human follow-up.\n2. **Strategy-level process gates pending human review** — Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, and Documentation Support are all \"Pending human review\" in Prerequisites & Process Gates. A base image swap is not expected to change transitive dependency licensing (same content, PQC-enabled OpenSSL), but the pending Licence Validation gate is recorded as an unfavorable human-process signal on the campaign epics.\n3. **Documentation Support ambiguity** — listed as \"Pending human review\", not \"Yes\", so no `docs-authoring` epic is included (Rule 11 not triggered). PQC is transparent to users (UXD: Not applicable). If human review confirms documentation support is required, a `docs-authoring` epic should be added at revision.\n4. **NFR vs Technical Approach tension on fms-guardrails-orchestrator** — the Security NFR mandates ML-KEM hybrid support after migration, while the Technical Approach treats an OCP exception as the fallback if the September 15 deadline cannot be met. The decomposition models the migration as gated work (E006) with the exception path recorded as the gate fallback; the exception filing itself is out of scope (each team files its own).\n5. **Priority note** — every epic is P0, matching an all-P0 HLR set under a hard deadline; this is a compliance mandate, not a priority collapse. The only non-P0 strategy item (ML-DSA certificates) is explicitly deferred and excluded.\n6. **TrustyAI explainability service** — expected to file an OCP exception (Java/NSS, not OpenSSL; JDK 29 timeline September 2027). No switch epic is created for it (its base is `ubi9/openjdk-17-runtime`, which has no PQC path; the filing is the team's own out-of-scope work). It is accounted for through E009's readiness matrix and zero-unaccounted-images tracking.\n7. **Scope trap addressed** — AC-1's \"measured by automated CI scan across the RHOAI 3.6 payload\" implies CI infrastructure that does not exist yet; E010 provisions it. FIPS+PQC incompatibility on FIPS-mode clusters is expected behavior for this release and is encoded as an exclusion in E010's acceptance criteria rather than a compliance failure.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nThe AIPCC PQC base image variants (python/nodejs) and the RHEL 9.8 rebase are external dependencies with uncertain timing. Partial value is deliverable without them, so the strategy splits:\n\n- **Tier 1 (independent, start immediately)**: E002 (ubi9-minimal family, ~68 images — confirmed-available target base), E005+E006 (guardrails TLS migration), E007+E008 (trainer data cache), E009 (readiness matrix and coordination), E010 (CI scan), E011 (performance threshold and methodology), E001 itself (risk-reduction confirmation).\n- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated)**: E003 (~34 rhel9-ELS images, gated on the RHEL 9.8 rebase committed date), E004 (~11 python/nodejs images, gated on PQC variant availability).\n\nIf a Tier 2 dependency does not resolve in time, the affected component teams file OCP exceptions by September 15 (tracked by E009); the Tier 1 value — the bulk of the payload on `ubi9-minimal-pqc` — still ships.\n"}}],"model":"zai-org/GLM-5.3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":100252,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-abfbe2b1297d6a9a","agent_id":"aad452a5752ce3ae8","session_id":"ffc190d1-a457-4608-ad90-0fad47057ac1","uuid":"95c89033-d8eb-4599-825d-7296d86df0ff","timestamp":"2026-10-09T14:20:17.207Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8001"}
