he migration before the September 15, 2026 deadline.\n- Verify backwards compatibility requirements: PQC hybrid key exchange is negotiated opportunistically — non-PQC TLS 1.3 clients must continue working via classic ECDHE.\n- Record the recommended path and the fallback determination (migrate vs. file an OCP exception documenting the TLS stack and remediation plan).\n\nOut of scope: executing the migration (follow-on remediation work); the orchestrator's ubi9-minimal base image switch (part of the platform-wide switch campaign, which delivers platform consistency but not ML-KEM for this component); Go ML-KEM and TLSAdherence (RHAISTRAT-7816); ML-DSA certificates (deferred).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- At least one concrete migration path to ML-KEM hybrid key exchange support is documented, with the specific TLS stack changes it requires (dependencies, feature flags, configuration surfaces).\n- Each path is assessed for backwards compatibility with non-PQC TLS 1.3 clients (classic ECDHE fallback preserved).\n- Each path is assessed for effect on the service's optional TLS/mTLS and per-service downstream TLS configuration behavior — no reduction in existing TLS capabilities without explicit documentation.\n- An effort estimate and September 15 feasibility determination is recorded for the recommended path.\n- A recommendation is published: proceed with migration, or file an OCP exception with a remediation plan that includes the component's TLS stack and the reason it cannot negotiate ML-KEM.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **[P0] All RHOAI runtime images must use a PQC-enabled base image... for RHOAI 3.6 / OCP 5.0** — this component cannot be remediated by a base image swap alone; the spike establishes the alternative route to ML-KEM compliance (or the documented exception rationale).\n- Supports the strategy's Security NFR: fms-guardrails-orchestrator must support ML-KEM (FIPS 203) hybrid key exchange after migration.\n- Supports strategy AC-2: exception filings must document the component's TLS stack and reason it cannot negotiate ML-KEM — the spike produces exactly that documentation.\n\n## Open Questions Addressed\n\n- Resolves the strategy's named unknown for fms-guardrails-orchestrator (rustls+ring TLS stack, TLS migration spike RHOAIENG-7452) — one of the three components listed as likely needing OCP exceptions.\n"}}],"model":"zai-org/GLM-5.3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":109252,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-abfbe2b1297d6a9a","agent_id":"aad452a5752ce3ae8","session_id":"ffc190d1-a457-4608-ad90-0fad47057ac1","uuid":"9090636e-a45c-4827-a926-6389f25efe89","timestamp":"2026-10-09T14:21:48.752Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8001"}
