{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b37f81fa2ff87e54","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8001-E007\n3\ttitle: Audit trainer data cache Rust TLS posture\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8001\n5\tcomponent: Training Kubeflow\n6\tteam: Trainer team\n7\ttype: Investigation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tinvestigation_signals:\n10\t  question_specificity: 1\n11\t  source_accessibility: 1\n12\t  local_runnability: 1\n13\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: 0\n14\t  human_judgment_required: 0\n15\timplementation_type: null\n16\tdependencies: []\n17\tai_signals: null\n18\tai_implementability: High\n19\tai_implementability_score: 3\n20\tjira_key: null\n21\tbranch: null\n22\tgated_by: null\n23\tgate_failure_impact: null\n24\t---\n25\t# Audit trainer data cache Rust TLS posture\n26\t\n27\t## Description\n28\t\n29\tThis investigation (the strategy's audit spike, RHOAIENG-7196) determines the TLS posture of the trainer data cache: which TLS stack it uses, whether it is a pure-Rust stack (rustls+ring or similar) or an OpenSSL-backed stack (native-tls), and therefore whether it can inherit ML-KEM support from a PQC-enabled base image or requires TLS stack remediation per the strategy's Security NFR.\n30\t\n31\tThe strategy lists trainer data cache among the components likely needing OCP exceptions, with its Rust TLS posture explicitly unknown. The audit's outcome determines whether remediation work exists: a pure-Rust TLS stack requires migration to ML-KEM hybrid support (or an exception filing); an OpenSSL-backed stack inherits PQC from the base image switch and needs no remediation.\n32\t\n33\t## Scope\n34\t\n35\t- Enumerate the trainer data cache's TLS-related dependencies and configuration (TLS libraries, crypto providers, feature flags) from the trainer component's source and lockfile.\n36\t- Determine whether the data cache's TLS connections (inbound and outbound) use system OpenSSL (native-tls or equivalent) or a pure-Rust TLS stack (rustls+ring or similar).\n37\t- Assess whether the current stack can negotiate ML-KEM hybrid key exchange as-is, and if not, what migration would be required (scope and effort estimate).\n38\t- Record the determination: remediation required (with recommended path and effort) or no remediation needed (base image switch suffices).\n39\t- If remediation is required but cannot land before September 15, 2026: produce the documentation needed for the trainer team's OCP exception filing — the component's TLS stack and the reason it cannot negotiate ML-KEM.\n40\t\n41\tOut of scope: executing any remediation (follow-on gated work); the trainer component's base image switch (platform-wide switch campaign); Go ML-KEM and TLS-scanner CI (RHAISTRAT-7816); ML-DSA certificates (deferred).\n42\t\n43\t## Acceptance Criteria\n44\t\n45\t- The trainer data cache's TLS stack is identified with evidence (dependencies, providers, configuration) — pure-Rust vs OpenSSL-backed is deterministically recorded.\n46\t- If the stack is pure-Rust: a remediation path with scope and effort estimate is documented, or an OCP exception rationale (TLS stack, reason ML-KEM cannot be negotiated, remediation plan) is produced for the trainer team to file.\n47\t- If the stack is OpenSSL-backed: it is recorded that the base image switch delivers PQC compliance for this component and no TLS remediation is needed.\n48\t- The determination is published to the PQC readiness matrix and the trainer team's tracking ticket linked to the parent strategy.\n49\t\n50\t## HLR Traceability\n51\t\n52\t- **[P0] All RHOAI runtime images must use a PQC-enabled base image... for RHOAI 3.6 / OCP 5.0** — resolves whether the base image switch is sufficient for this component or whether it joins the exception/remediation path.\n53\t- Supports the strategy's Security NFR: any Rust components using pure-Rust TLS stacks must support ML-KEM (FIPS 203) hybrid key exchange after migration — the audit establishes whether this component is in that class.\n54\t- Supports strategy AC-2: exception filings must document the component's TLS stack and reason it cannot negotiate ML-KEM — the audit produces that documentation if needed.\n55\t\n56\t## Open Questions Addressed\n57\t\n58\t- Resolves the strategy's named unknown: \"trainer data cache — Rust TLS posture unknown; audit spike RHOAIENG-7196\".\n59\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b6872f863486b2c4","agent_id":"a38f3d0c7666b3a93","session_id":"ffc190d1-a457-4608-ad90-0fad47057ac1","uuid":"3a22cc1d-ce50-415d-8fd1-55fb14509859","timestamp":"2026-10-09T14:27:09.005Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b37f81fa2ff87e54","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8001 decomposition"}
