{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-928151d7dbb8d671","type":"tool_result","content":"16:**Detailed**: The FMS Guardrails Orchestrator is a Rust service built on axum and tonic that provides a unified REST API for safe AI text generation. It accepts client requests on v1 and v2 API surfaces (port 8033/TCP), orchestrates content safety detection by forwarding text to configurable detector services via REST, chunks text via gRPC chunker services, and coordinates generation through a TGIS or caikit-nlp gRPC backend. A separate health server (port 8034/TCP) provides unauthenticated health and info endpoints. The service supports optional TLS and mTLS on its ingress surface, passthrough of configurable client headers to downstream services, and rewriting of X-Forwarded-Access-Token headers to Authorization Bearer for downstream authentication. Two additional v2 endpoints for OpenAI-compatible chat and text completions detection are conditionally enabled when an OpenAI-compatible generation backend is configured. The orchestrator is built for multi-architecture deployment (amd64, ppc64le, s390x) on UBI 9 minimal base images. [source: Cargo.toml:1-7, config/config.yaml:1-70, src/server/routes.rs:62-108, src/args.rs:31-47]\n20:The orchestrator follows a middleware proxy pattern: it sits between API clients and multiple backend services (generation, detection, chunking), adding safety guardrails to the text generation pipeline. The axum HTTP framework handles inbound REST requests on port 8033, while tonic-generated gRPC clients communicate with downstream TGIS generation and caikit chunker services. Detector services are reached via reqwest REST clients. Each downstream service type supports independent TLS configuration through a named TLS map in the YAML config, allowing per-service certificate and CA bundles. [source: config/config.yaml:53-66, Cargo.toml:56-65, 85-89]\n24:Authentication is passthrough-based rather than enforced: configurable header names are forwarded from client requests to downstream services, and an optional X-Forwarded-Access-Token rewrite converts forwarded access tokens into Authorization Bearer headers for downstream consumption. The orchestrator itself does not validate tokens or enforce authorization policies; it delegates authentication to the downstream services and to the platform's ingress layer. TLS and mTLS on the inbound surface are optional, controlled by CLI flags and environment variables. [source: src/server/routes.rs:452-480, src/args.rs:43-47, config/config.yaml:67-70]\n26:The cryptographic stack uses rustls with the ring provider throughout (rustls, hyper-rustls, tokio-rustls, tonic TLS). Ring is not a FIPS-validated cryptographic provider, which means the service cannot claim FIPS compliance for its TLS operations. OpenSSL is present only in the build stage for protobuf compilation and is not installed in the release image. [source: Cargo.toml:33-34, 62-65, 81-83, 85-88, Dockerfile.konflux:17, 51-53]\n68:| /api/v1/task/classification-with-text-generation | POST | 8033/TCP | HTTP/HTTPS | Unknown | TLS 1.2+ (optional) | Passthrough headers |  | classification with gen |\n69:| /api/v1/task/server-streaming-classification-with-text-generation | POST | 8033/TCP | HTTP/HTTPS | Unknown | TLS 1.2+ (optional) | Passthrough headers |  | stream classification with gen |\n70:| /api/v2/chat/completions-detection | POST | 8033/TCP | HTTP/HTTPS | Unknown | TLS 1.2+ (optional) | Passthrough headers |  | chat completions detection |\n71:| /api/v2/text/completions-detection | POST | 8033/TCP | HTTP/HTTPS | Unknown | TLS 1.2+ (optional) | Passthrough headers |  | completions detection |\n72:| /api/v2/text/detection/chat | POST | 8033/TCP | HTTP/HTTPS | Unknown | TLS 1.2+ (optional) | Passthrough headers |  | detect chat |\n73:| /api/v2/text/detection/content | POST | 8033/TCP | HTTP/HTTPS | Unknown | TLS 1.2+ (optional) | Passthrough headers |  | detection content |\n74:| /api/v2/text/detection/context | POST | 8033/TCP | HTTP/HTTPS | Unknown | TLS 1.2+ (optional) | Passthrough headers |  | detect context documents |\n75:| /api/v2/text/detection/generated | POST | 8033/TCP | HTTP/HTTPS | Unknown | TLS 1.2+ (optional) | Passthrough headers |  | detect generated |\n76:| /api/v2/text/detection/stream-content | POST | 8033/TCP | HTTP/HTTPS | Unknown | TLS 1.2+ (optional) | Passthrough headers |  | stream content detection |\n77:| /api/v2/text/generation-detection | POST | 8033/TCP | HTTP/HTTPS | Unknown | TLS 1.2+ (optional) | Passthrough headers |  | generation with detection |\n108:| hyper-rustls | 0.27.7 | Yes | Unknown | Cargo package dependency |\n===TRAINER===\n16:**Detailed**: The trainer component is the Kubeflow Training operator for RHOAI, implementing a controller-runtime-based Kubernetes operator that reconciles TrainJob, TrainingRuntime, and ClusterTrainingRuntime custom resources. It orchestrates distributed ML training workloads by translating high-level training specifications into JobSet resources, with pluggable runtime plugins for different training frameworks (MPI, Flux) and gang scheduling coordinators (Kubernetes Scheduler Plugins CoScheduling, Volcano). The operator runs as a single Deployment (`kubeflow-trainer-controller-manager`) exposing webhook, metrics, and status-server ports via a ClusterIP Service. It includes admission webhooks that validate and default TrainJob, TrainingRuntime, and ClusterTrainingRuntime resources. The component also ships dataset and model initializer containers as Python entrypoints that use HuggingFace Hub and OpenDAL for data retrieval, plus a Python SDK (`kubeflow_trainer_api`) for programmatic training job management. On OpenShift, the operator reads the cluster's TLS security profile from `config.openshift.io/v1/apiservers` to apply consistent cipher suite configuration across its webhook and metrics servers, gracefully falling back to hardened Intermediate defaults on non-OpenShift clusters. RHOAI-specific additions include RBAC aggregation roles (training-admin, training-edit, training-view), NetworkPolicy management per TrainJob, and training progression status monitoring. [source: cmd/trainer-controller-manager/main.go:74-176, pkg/tls/tls.go:93-144, pkg/rhai/networkpolicy.go:152-180, pkg/rhai/progression/progression.go:147, manifests/rhoai/kubeflow-training-roles.yaml:45]\n28:TLS configuration is centralized through `pkg/tls`, which dynamically reads the OpenShift cluster TLS security profile and applies it uniformly to metrics, webhook, and status servers. The fallback to hardened Intermediate defaults (TLS 1.2+, AEAD-only ciphers) ensures secure operation on non-OpenShift clusters. [source: pkg/tls/tls.go:93-144, cmd/trainer-controller-manager/main.go:119-128]\n81:| /mutate-trainer-kubeflow-org-v1alpha1-trainjob | POST |  | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS | Kubernetes admission |  | Mutating admission webhook |\n83:| /validate-trainer-kubeflow-org-v1alpha1-clustertrainingruntime | POST |  | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS | Kubernetes admission |  | Validating admission webhook |\n84:| /validate-trainer-kubeflow-org-v1alpha1-trainingruntime | POST |  | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS | Kubernetes admission |  | Validating admission webhook |\n85:| /validate-trainer-kubeflow-org-v1alpha1-trainjob | POST |  | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS | Kubernetes admission |  | Validating admission webhook |\n136:| OpenShift Cluster TLS Configuration | Resource Read | runtime-configuration | Read cluster TLS security profile to configure webhook and metrics server cipher suites |\n150:| Name | Type | Hosts | Port | Protocol | Encryption | TLS Mode | Exposure |\n157:| Kubernetes API | 6443 | HTTPS/WSS | TLS 1.2+ | ServiceAccount | Kubernetes resource operations |\n216:| tls-config | crypto/tls | TLS configuration import | dependency-signal |\n227:| **OpenSSL in image** | Yes (via UBI 9 minimal base) | cmd/trainer-controller-manager/Dockerfile.rhoai.konflux:19 |\n234:| **TLS configuration** | Uses crypto/tls with OpenShift cluster TLS profile; falls back to Intermediate (TLS 1.2+, AEAD ciphers) | pkg/tls/tls.go:39-60, 166-170 |\n235:| **Crypto libraries** | stdlib crypto/tls (FIPS via OpenSSL with strictfipsruntime) | go.mod, pkg/tls/tls.go:22 |\n236:| **Certificate handling** | cert-controller for webhook certs; system trust store for API server TLS | cmd/trainer-controller-manager/main.go:146-158 |\n237:| **Non-FIPS crypto risks** | None detected; all cipher suites are FIPS-approved AEAD ciphers (AES-GCM, ChaCha20-Poly1305) | pkg/tls/tls.go:53-60 |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b6872f863486b2c4","agent_id":"a38f3d0c7666b3a93","session_id":"ffc190d1-a457-4608-ad90-0fad47057ac1","uuid":"7d8581b9-bd5a-451b-83e3-4274b0b1d32a","timestamp":"2026-10-09T14:31:08.361Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-928151d7dbb8d671","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8001 decomposition"}
