{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b44684030fec0387","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8001\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n9\t  description: E001 and E007 acceptance criteria require publishing outcomes to the\n10\t    PQC readiness matrix created by E009 with no DAG edge; the tracker-absorbs parallel\n11\t    model is rule-justified (Rules 14/15) but the matrix-creation-before-publication\n12\t    ordering is only implicit\n13\t- severity: minor\n14\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n15\t  description: E007/E008 signal rationales assert Cargo manifests/lockfiles exist\n16\t    in the trainer repo; the trainer architecture context describes a Go operator\n17\t    with Python initializers (opendal as a Python package), so the Rust build-file\n18\t    premise is unconfirmed — the audit question itself remains sound\n19\terror: null\n20\t---\n21\t## Review Summary\n22\t\n23\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n24\tRecommendation: accept\n25\t\n26\t## Criterion Details\n27\t\n28\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n29\t\n30\tTraceability matrix present in the decomposition summary and verified against each epic's HLR Traceability section. All three P0 HLRs are covered:\n31\t\n32\t- HLR-1 (PQC-enabled base images) — E002/E003/E004 deliver the switch for the three base-image families; E001 is the gating investigation for the two unconfirmed families; E006/E008 deliver the non-OpenSSL components' route to ML-KEM compliance; E010 provides verification.\n33\t- HLR-2 (OCP exception filing) — E009 enforces and tracks the exception process to the zero-unaccounted-images outcome; E006/E008 record the exception path as their gate fallbacks.\n34\t- HLR-3 (runtime only, final stage only) — encoded as a scope constraint and an explicit acceptance criterion on each campaign epic.\n35\t- Deferred HLR (ML-DSA certificates) — explicitly excluded with justification, matching the strategy's [Deferred] marking.\n36\t\n37\tPriority inheritance is clean: all epics are P0, matching an all-P0 non-deferred HLR set under a hard September 15, 2026 deadline; every gate (E001→E003/E004, E005→E006, E007→E008) is P0→P0. No priority collapse — the only non-P0 strategy item (ML-DSA) is excluded, not bundled. TrustyAI (openjdk-17-runtime, \"Exception expected\" in the strategy's own mapping table) is correctly handled: no switch epic (the strategy provides no PQC path for that base), accounted for through E009's readiness matrix and zero-unaccounted-images tracking, with the filing itself out of scope per the strategy.\n38\t\n39\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n40\t\n41\tCross-check passed: all four diagram edges (E001→E003, E001→E004, E005→E006, E007→E008) match the target epics' frontmatter `dependencies` exactly, with no extra frontmatter dependencies, no reversed arrows, and a correct roots list (E001, E002, E005, E007, E009, E010, E011). No circular dependencies; all references point to existing epics; no `-BRANCH-` files exist (the gated-epic mechanism was chosen instead, with the conditional-decomposition decision documented and reasoned).\n42\t\n43\tEvery edge is a strategy-grounded Rule 3 investigation gate: the strategy's Dependencies section itself states the AIPCC RHEL 9.8 rebase \"gates RHEL9-ELS image adoption\" (E001→E003, `rewrite`) and that python/nodejs PQC variants are unconfirmed (E001→E004, `remove`); E005→E006 and E007→E008 gate approach/existence on the strategy's named spikes (RHOAIENG-7452, RHOAIENG-7196). None of the edges is justified by an image digest/SHA/RELATED_IMAGE — the payload-consumption relationships (campaigns → E010 scan, campaigns → E011 measurement) are correctly modeled as acceptance-criteria gates per Rule 13, not `dependencies` edges, and the decomposition states this justification explicitly. No konflux-onboarding, license-validation, or repo-onboarding epics exist, so the rule 7/8/10 parallelism constraints are vacuously satisfied.\n44\t\n45\tParallel work is correctly preserved: E002 (confirmed-available target base) correctly has no edge to E001; E009/E010/E011 correctly run parallel to the campaigns; the guardrails/trainer TLS migrations are correctly not coupled to the base image switch (rustls+ring does not use system OpenSSL). Critical path length 2 is within the S-strategy heuristic (1-2).\n46\t\n47\tMinor observation: E001's and E007's acceptance criteria require outcomes \"published to the PQC readiness matrix,\" an artifact E009 creates, with no DAG edge. The decomposition's documented tracker-absorbs model (Rules 14/15 — coordination does not consume build artifacts; matrix entries authored as TBD and updated as outcomes resolve) makes this acceptable, but the sequencing (matrix creation precedes first publication) is implicit rather than stated.\n48\t\n49\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n50\t\n51\tAll components are canonical (verified against `.context/rhai-components.txt`): AIPCC Productization, AI Core Platform, Notebooks Images, Model Runtimes, AI Guardrails, Training Kubeflow, DevOps, CI/CD, PerfScale. Team assignments match the strategy's Impacted Teams: DevOps/Build coordinates base image pipeline updates (E002-E004, E009, E010), the AIPCC team confirms variant availability (E001), AI Guardrails and Trainer teams own their remediations (E005-E008), and the Performance team owns the measurement (E011).\n52\t\n53\tThe three campaign epics (E002/E003/E004) span many owning teams' images, but they are the \"same logical change\" — an identical final-runtime-stage swap to one named target base — coordinated by a single team, with per-team execution happening in tracking tickets below epic level. This is exactly the structure the strategy (self-described as a compliance coordination tracker) prescribes, and the non-obvious component mappings are documented with rationale in the decomposition's component mapping notes.\n54\t\n55\tNo scope duplication: E002/E003/E004 have disjoint image families; E009's payload-wide readiness matrix is the single aggregation point fed by the campaigns' per-family tracking (hierarchical decomposition of AC-2, not duplicate data structures); E010 provisions the scan that the campaigns' ACs reference (consumer, not rebuild). No work-product type mixing: code implementation (E002-E004, E006, E008), CI tooling (E010), coordination artifact (E009), and benchmarking (E011) are separate epics. Epic sizes are consistent with the strategy's S effort estimate — the campaigns' own work is pipeline updates plus tracking, not hands-on modification of ~68/~34/~11 images.\n56\t\n57\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n58\t\n59\tAll three Investigations genuinely resolve uncertainty that changes downstream structure: E001 gates E004's existence (`remove`) and rewrites E003's scope (`rewrite`); E005's chosen migration path rewrites E006's approach; E007's audit determines whether E008 exists at all (`remove`). Each has downstream dependents. All eight Implementations produce concrete artifacts (switched images, migrated TLS stacks, readiness matrix, CI scan job, threshold plus benchmark report).\n60\t\n61\tE011 is correctly typed Implementation rather than Investigation: the numeric threshold decision does not change which epics exist (the decomposition's health warning #1 reasons this explicitly — measurement proceeds either way), so the threshold is a deliverable/AC within the epic, not a structural gate.\n62\t\n63\tGating metadata is complete and correct on all four gated epics: `gated_by` set, `gate_failure_impact` with both `action` and `fallback_approach` populated, actions matching failure semantics (`rewrite` for scope/approach changes, `remove` for existence gates), and every `gated_by` target present in the epic's direct `dependencies`. No epic lists an Investigation dependency without `gated_by`; no `gated_by` exists without a populated failure impact.\n64\t\n65\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n66\t\n67\tType-appropriate signal fields throughout: E001/E005/E007 carry `investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric) with `ai_signals: null`; all eight Implementations carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric) with `investigation_signals: null`. All 11 ai-signals rationale files exist, and every frontmatter value matches its rationale file (verified signal-by-signal).\n68\t\n69\tSignal values are consistent with strategy content: `open_questions` is +1 where targets are confirmed and the change is mechanical (E002, E009, E010) and -1 where the strategy flags unresolved gates (E003 rebase date, E004 variant availability, E006/E008 approach/existence, E011 threshold). E001's investigation signals honestly capture that the answers are pending AIPCC human decisions (source_accessibility 0, human_judgment_required -2) rather than claim readability that does not exist. E005/E007 source_accessibility +1 is justified by readable repositories and public rustls/provider documentation.\n70\t\n71\tDistinctions that could look inconsistent across sibling epics are explicitly reasoned in the rationales and are defensible under the rubric: E003 external_dependency 0 (target image already published; the rebase is an internal-team timing dependency, captured in open_questions -1) versus E004's -1 (the variants do not exist and must be delivered by AIPCC); the campaigns' human_process_gates -1 (multi-team human ticket coordination plus pending strategy-level gates) versus E006/E008's 0 (single-repo code work with standard review).\n72\t\n73\tMinor observation: E007/E008 signal rationales assert \"Cargo manifests/lockfile\" exist in the trainer repository; the trainer architecture context describes a Go operator with Python initializer entrypoints (opendal listed as a Python package dependency). The audit question itself remains sound — the strategy poses the Rust TLS posture as unknown, and reading the repository resolves it either way — but the rationale presumes Rust build files the architecture context does not confirm.\n74\t\n75\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n76\t\n77\tEvery epic has testable acceptance criteria with concrete verification: exact commands and expected values (`update-crypto-policies --show` → `DEFAULT:PQ`; `openssl list -kem-algorithms` → ML-KEM-512/768/1024), deadlines (September 15, 2026 for compliance outcomes; Sprint 2 for the threshold decision), and countable outcomes (zero unaccounted-for images per family and payload-wide).\n78\t\n79\tRule-mandated ACs are present wherever applicable: rollback ACs on all replacement/migration epics (E002/E003/E004 base swaps revertible by restoring the prior base reference and rebuilding; E006/E008 TLS changes revertible by restoring the prior stack configuration); \"build pipelines produce green builds\" on all three campaigns; the FIPS-mode exclusion from the strategy's AC-1 note encoded in E009 and E010. No docs-authoring or konflux-chain epics exist, so their mandated ACs are not applicable (Documentation Support is \"Pending human review\", not \"Yes\" — Rule 11 correctly not triggered and flagged as health warning #3 with a revision path).\n80\t\n81\tNo epic body references sibling epics by draft ID (grep-verified across all 11 bodies); cross-epic relationships are described by name (\"the platform-wide switch campaign\", \"the AIPCC confirmation work\"), which survives Jira import.\n82\t\n83\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n84\t\n85\tAll seven rows of the strategy's base image mapping table are accounted for: the three switch families (E002/E003/E004), the python/nodejs TBD variants (E001 gate plus E004, covering python-311/312/312-minimal and the nodejs-22 dashboard), and the TrustyAI exception row (E009 tracking, matching the strategy's own \"Exception expected\" and its out-of-scope exception-filing boundary).\n86\t\n87\tAll three strategy acceptance criteria are allocated: AC-1 → E010 (the \"measured by automated CI scan\" clause) plus the campaigns' per-family pass criteria; AC-2 → E009 plus the campaigns (switched-or-exceptioned accounting, zero unaccounted); AC-3 → E009 (tracking tickets linked to the parent strategy). All three likely-exception components are covered: fms-guardrails-orchestrator (E005+E006), TrustyAI (E009 accounting, grounded in its architecture context confirming the Java/Quarkus/OpenJDK-17 posture), trainer data cache (E007+E008).\n88\t\n89\tAll four NFRs are addressed: Security (E005-E008 per the Security NFR for non-Go components), Backwards compatibility (classic-ECDHE fallback ACs in E006/E008, assumption carried in E002), Availability (no-HA-change ACs across campaigns, migrations, and E011), Performance (E011 resolves the NEEDS DOMAIN INPUT threshold and delivers the before-ship measurement required by the Performance Team Support prerequisite).\n90\t\n91\tStrategy-level context is carried forward rather than dropped: all three strategy risks appear in the owning epics' risk sections; assumptions are carried (E002, E010); open questions map to E001/E005/E007/E009; the \"to be created\" PQC Readiness Matrix design doc is E009's deliverable; the weekly reminder and leadership-escalation cadence is E009's scope; the FIPS-mode exclusion is encoded in E009/E010; and every epic's out-of-scope section mirrors the strategy's boundaries (RHAISTRAT-7816 ownership, post-5.0 evolution, ML-DSA, per-team exception filing).\n92\t\n93\tConditional outcomes are covered via the gate mechanism (`rewrite`/`remove` with documented fallback approaches) rather than branch files — a coherent, explicitly documented choice given that the negative outcomes (exception filing) are themselves out of strategy scope and produce no RHOAI epics. Documentation Support is \"Pending human review\" rather than \"Yes\", so no docs-authoring epic is required; the decomposition correctly flags this for human follow-up instead of silently assuming either answer.\n94\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-too