{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b8dd670d9f790207","type":"tool_result","content":"RHAISTRAT-8001-E001.md:34:Both questions are attributed to the same AIPCC coordination effort and produce one deliverable, so they are combined into a single investigation. The answers determine whether the affected image families proceed with base image switches or fall back to OCP exception filings, and they populate the target-base entries of the PQC readiness matrix.\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E001.md:43:- Communicate outcomes to the component teams whose images depend on each answer, and to the PQC readiness matrix.\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E007.md:48:- The determination is published to the PQC readiness matrix and the trainer team's tracking ticket linked to the parent strategy.\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E010.md:40:- Produce a per-image and aggregated compliance report across the payload, consumable by the PQC readiness matrix and compliance coordination tracking.\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E009.md:3:title: PQC readiness matrix and compliance coordination\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E009.md:29:# PQC readiness matrix and compliance coordination\n=== Rust/Cargo premises ===\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E002.md:44:- Includes images of components whose TLS stack is not OpenSSL-backed (e.g., the Rust guardrails orchestrator, whose TLS remediation is tracked separately): the base switch here delivers platform consistency and system-OpenSSL compliance, while those components' ML-KEM negotiation capability is resolved by their own remediation work or OCP exceptions.\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E005-ai-signals.md:7:| question_specificity | +1 | Concrete question with a defined criterion: which migration path lets the rustls+ring stack negotiate ML-KEM hybrid key exchange (e.g., X25519MLKEM768) while preserving optional TLS/mTLS and per-service downstream TLS configuration; pass/fail is demonstrable by a test handshake. |\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E005-ai-signals.md:8:| source_accessibility | +1 | The answer is determinable from readable artifacts: the fms-guardrails-orchestrator repository (Cargo.toml feature flags, TLS configuration surfaces, documented in its architecture context file) plus public rustls/provider documentation. |\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E005-ai-signals.md:9:| local_runnability | +1 | The Rust service and its TLS stack build and run locally (cargo); TLS handshakes can be exercised locally against a test client without any cluster. |\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E006-ai-signals.md:10:| external_dependency | 0 | Expected paths use public, released Rust crypto/TLS crates; no upstream contribution or vendor coordination is presumed. |\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E006-ai-signals.md:13:| architecture_claims | +1 | The strategy cites the component's architecture context (fms-guardrails-orchestrator.md: rustls+ring throughout, non-FIPS-validated provider, OpenSSL build-stage only) — the claim this epic acts on. |\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E005.md:30:This investigation (the strategy's TLS migration spike, RHOAIENG-7452) determines how the fms-guardrails-orchestrator service — which uses a rustls+ring TLS stack throughout (rustls, hyper-rustls, tokio-rustls, tonic TLS with the ring provider, per its architecture context) — can be migrated to support ML-KEM (FIPS 203) hybrid key exchange, per the strategy's Security NFR requiring non-Go components with pure-Rust TLS stacks to support ML-KEM hybrid key exchange after migration.\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E005.md:32:A base image swap cannot remediate this component: rustls+ring does not use system OpenSSL, so it inherits nothing from a PQC-enabled base image. The spike's outcome determines the migration approach and effort for the remediation work that follows, and informs whether the team can meet the September 15 deadline through migration or must file an OCP exception.\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E005.md:36:- Determine viable migration paths for the orchestrator's TLS stack to negotiate ML-KEM hybrid key exchange (e.g., X25519MLKEM768), evaluating options against the current rustls+ring dependency set (rustls, hyper-rustls, tokio-rustls, tonic TLS features as recorded in the component's architecture context).\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E005.md:60:- Resolves the strategy's named unknown for fms-guardrails-orchestrator (rustls+ring TLS stack, TLS migration spike RHOAIENG-7452) — one of the three components listed as likely needing OCP exceptions.\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E006.md:40:The base image switch coordinated in the platform-wide campaign delivers platform consistency for this component's image but does not deliver ML-KEM — rustls+ring does not use system OpenSSL — so this migration is the component's actual route to PQC compliance.\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E006.md:63:- **[P0] All RHOAI runtime images must use a PQC-enabled base image... for RHOAI 3.6 / OCP 5.0** — delivers this component's route to ML-KEM compliance that the base image switch cannot provide (rustls+ring is not OpenSSL-backed).\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E007-ai-signals.md:7:| question_specificity | +1 | Concrete, enumerable question with a deterministic criterion: which TLS libraries and crypto providers does the trainer data cache use (pure-Rust rustls+ring vs OpenSSL-backed native-tls), read from its dependencies and configuration; the outcome classifies the component and determines whether remediation work exists. |\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E007-ai-signals.md:8:| source_accessibility | +1 | The answer is fully determinable from readable artifacts: the trainer repository (source, Cargo manifests/lockfile, TLS configuration) and its architecture context file. |\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E007-ai-signals.md:9:| local_runnability | +1 | The trainer component builds and runs locally (Go + Rust + Python codebases); TLS behavior can be exercised locally with a test client if dynamic confirmation is needed beyond static analysis. |\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E008-ai-signals.md:9:| open_questions | -1 | The defining open question (Rust TLS posture, RHOAIENG-7196) gates this epic's existence — unresolved until the audit completes. |\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E008-ai-signals.md:10:| external_dependency | 0 | Expected remediation uses public, released Rust crypto/TLS crates; no upstream contribution or vendor coordination presumed. |\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E007.md:3:title: Audit trainer data cache Rust TLS posture\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E007.md:25:# Audit trainer data cache Rust TLS posture\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E007.md:29:This investigation (the strategy's audit spike, RHOAIENG-7196) determines the TLS posture of the trainer data cache: which TLS stack it uses, whether it is a pure-Rust stack (rustls+ring or similar) or an OpenSSL-backed stack (native-tls), and therefore whether it can inherit ML-KEM support from a PQC-enabled base image or requires TLS stack remediation per the strategy's Security NFR.\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E007.md:31:The strategy lists trainer data cache among the components likely needing OCP exceptions, with its Rust TLS posture explicitly unknown. The audit's outcome determines whether remediation work exists: a pure-Rust TLS stack requires migration to ML-KEM hybrid support (or an exception filing); an OpenSSL-backed stack inherits PQC from the base image switch and needs no remediation.\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E007.md:36:- Determine whether the data cache's TLS connections (inbound and outbound) use system OpenSSL (native-tls or equivalent) or a pure-Rust TLS stack (rustls+ring or similar).\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E007.md:45:- The trainer data cache's TLS stack is identified with evidence (dependencies, providers, configuration) — pure-Rust vs OpenSSL-backed is deterministically recorded.\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E007.md:46:- If the stack is pure-Rust: a remediation path with scope and effort estimate is documented, or an OCP exception rationale (TLS stack, reason ML-KEM cannot be negotiated, remediation plan) is produced for the trainer team to file.\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E007.md:53:- Supports the strategy's Security NFR: any Rust components using pure-Rust TLS stacks must support ML-KEM (FIPS 203) hybrid key exchange after migration — the audit establishes whether this component is in that class.\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E007.md:58:- Resolves the strategy's named unknown: \"trainer data cache — Rust TLS posture unknown; audit spike RHOAIENG-7196\".\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E008.md:39:This epic remediates the trainer data cache's TLS stack to support ML-KEM (FIPS 203) hybrid key exchange — but only if the TLS posture audit (RHOAIENG-7196) determines the component uses a pure-Rust TLS stack that cannot inherit PQC from a base image. If the audit finds an OpenSSL-backed stack, no remediation is needed (the platform-wide base image switch delivers PQC compliance for the component) and this epic is removed.\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E008.md:41:Per the strategy's Security NFR, Rust components using pure-Rust TLS stacks must support ML-KEM hybrid key exchange after migration. If the remediation cannot land before the September 15, 2026 deadline, the trainer team files an OCP exception via OCPEXCEPT with a remediation plan instead.\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E008.md:64:- Supports the strategy's Security NFR (ML-KEM hybrid for pure-Rust TLS stacks), Backwards compatibility NFR, and Availability NFR.\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E008.md:68:- Gated by the trainer data cache TLS posture audit (RHOAIENG-7196): if the audit finds an OpenSSL-backed stack, this epic is removed — no remediation is needed. If the audit finds a pure-Rust stack, this epic proceeds on the audit's recommended path.\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E009.md:41:- Track the exception path: components expected to file OCP exceptions (TrustyAI explainability service — Java/NSS, JDK 29 timeline September 2027; fms-guardrails-orchestrator if its TLS migration cannot land in time; trainer data cache if remediation cannot land in time; any python/nodejs-based component if PQC base variants are not available in time) each have a filed and acknowledged OCPEXCEPT exception with a remediation plan including the component's TLS stack and the reason it cannot negotiate ML-KEM.\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E009.md:44:- Update matrix entries as the AIPCC confirmation resolves python/nodejs target bases and as audits resolve Rust TLS postures.\nRHAISTRAT-8001-E010.md:41:- Encode the criterion's documented exclusions: the check applies to OpenSSL-backed components only — components using non-OpenSSL TLS stacks (rustls+ring, Java/NSS) are accounted for by OCP exception filings rather than this scan; on FIPS-mode clusters, FIPS policy deactivates PQC for this release, so `DEFAULT:PQ` non-functionality there is expected and not a compliance failure.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bfc0faf56a1d8564","agent_id":"aa60bfa32cd2e2564","session_id":"ffc190d1-a457-4608-ad90-0fad47057ac1","uuid":"e754a3f4-475b-4c44-aa5b-65b768f27377","timestamp":"2026-10-09T14:40:37.912Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b8dd670d9f790207","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8001 decomposition"}
