{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-87fe7340c7d4d523","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8001-E005\n3\ttitle: 'Spike: fms-guardrails-orchestrator TLS migration path to ML-KEM hybrid key\n4\t  exchange'\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8001\n6\tcomponent: AI Guardrails\n7\tteam: AI Guardrails team\n8\ttype: Investigation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tinvestigation_signals:\n11\t  question_specificity: 1\n12\t  source_accessibility: 1\n13\t  local_runnability: 1\n14\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: 0\n15\t  human_judgment_required: 0\n16\timplementation_type: null\n17\tdependencies: []\n18\tai_signals: null\n19\tai_implementability: High\n20\tai_implementability_score: 3\n21\tjira_key: null\n22\tbranch: null\n23\tgated_by: null\n24\tgate_failure_impact: null\n25\t---\n26\t# Spike: fms-guardrails-orchestrator TLS migration path to ML-KEM hybrid key exchange\n27\t\n28\t## Description\n29\t\n30\tThis investigation (the strategy's TLS migration spike, RHOAIENG-7452) determines how the fms-guardrails-orchestrator service — which uses a rustls+ring TLS stack throughout (rustls, hyper-rustls, tokio-rustls, tonic TLS with the ring provider, per its architecture context) — can be migrated to support ML-KEM (FIPS 203) hybrid key exchange, per the strategy's Security NFR requiring non-Go components with pure-Rust TLS stacks to support ML-KEM hybrid key exchange after migration.\n31\t\n32\tA base image swap cannot remediate this component: rustls+ring does not use system OpenSSL, so it inherits nothing from a PQC-enabled base image. The spike's outcome determines the migration approach and effort for the remediation work that follows, and informs whether the team can meet the September 15 deadline through migration or must file an OCP exception.\n33\t\n34\t## Scope\n35\t\n36\t- Determine viable migration paths for the orchestrator's TLS stack to negotiate ML-KEM hybrid key exchange (e.g., X25519MLKEM768), evaluating options against the current rustls+ring dependency set (rustls, hyper-rustls, tokio-rustls, tonic TLS features as recorded in the component's architecture context).\n37\t- Assess each path's compatibility with the service's TLS surfaces: optional inbound TLS/mTLS on the API server, per-service downstream TLS configuration (detector, chunker, generation clients), and the optional X-Forwarded-Access-Token rewrite behavior.\n38\t- Produce an effort estimate and feasibility assessment for landing the migration before the September 15, 2026 deadline.\n39\t- Verify backwards compatibility requirements: PQC hybrid key exchange is negotiated opportunistically — non-PQC TLS 1.3 clients must continue working via classic ECDHE.\n40\t- Record the recommended path and the fallback determination (migrate vs. file an OCP exception documenting the TLS stack and remediation plan).\n41\t\n42\tOut of scope: executing the migration (follow-on remediation work); the orchestrator's ubi9-minimal base image switch (part of the platform-wide switch campaign, which delivers platform consistency but not ML-KEM for this component); Go ML-KEM and TLSAdherence (RHAISTRAT-7816); ML-DSA certificates (deferred).\n43\t\n44\t## Acceptance Criteria\n45\t\n46\t- At least one concrete migration path to ML-KEM hybrid key exchange support is documented, with the specific TLS stack changes it requires (dependencies, feature flags, configuration surfaces).\n47\t- Each path is assessed for backwards compatibility with non-PQC TLS 1.3 clients (classic ECDHE fallback preserved).\n48\t- Each path is assessed for effect on the service's optional TLS/mTLS and per-service downstream TLS configuration behavior — no reduction in existing TLS capabilities without explicit documentation.\n49\t- An effort estimate and September 15 feasibility determination is recorded for the recommended path.\n50\t- A recommendation is published: proceed with migration, or file an OCP exception with a remediation plan that includes the component's TLS stack and the reason it cannot negotiate ML-KEM.\n51\t\n52\t## HLR Traceability\n53\t\n54\t- **[P0] All RHOAI runtime images must use a PQC-enabled base image... for RHOAI 3.6 / OCP 5.0** — this component cannot be remediated by a base image swap alone; the spike establishes the alternative route to ML-KEM compliance (or the documented exception rationale).\n55\t- Supports the strategy's Security NFR: fms-guardrails-orchestrator must support ML-KEM (FIPS 203) hybrid key exchange after migration.\n56\t- Supports strategy AC-2: exception filings must document the component's TLS stack and reason it cannot negotiate ML-KEM — the spike produces exactly that documentation.\n57\t\n58\t## Open Questions Addressed\n59\t\n60\t- Resolves the strategy's named unknown for fms-guardrails-orchestrator (rustls+ring TLS stack, TLS migration spike RHOAIENG-7452) — one of the three components listed as likely needing OCP exceptions.\n61\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a0166ce5350227c5","agent_id":"a9f7d0263db104ceb","session_id":"ffc190d1-a457-4608-ad90-0fad47057ac1","uuid":"570c032c-4539-42b8-80e5-cccaeee2a4e8","timestamp":"2026-10-09T14:43:32.239Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-87fe7340c7d4d523","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8001 decomposition"}
