{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8caccd4cc81c8e63","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8001-E010\n3\ttitle: Automated payload CI scan for PQC crypto policy compliance\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8001\n5\tcomponent: CI/CD\n6\tteam: DevOps/Build\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tai_signals:\n10\t  change_specificity: 1\n11\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n12\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n13\t  existing_foundation: 1\n14\t  open_questions: 1\n15\t  external_dependency: 0\n16\t  human_process_gates: 0\n17\t  repo_access: 1\n18\t  architecture_claims: 1\n19\timplementation_type: null\n20\tdependencies: []\n21\tinvestigation_signals: null\n22\tai_implementability: High\n23\tai_implementability_score: 6\n24\tjira_key: null\n25\tbranch: null\n26\tgated_by: null\n27\tgate_failure_impact: null\n28\t---\n29\t# Automated payload CI scan for PQC crypto policy compliance\n30\t\n31\t## Description\n32\t\n33\tThis epic builds the automated CI scan that measures the strategy's primary acceptance criterion: across the RHOAI 3.6 payload, every switched container reports `DEFAULT:PQ` as its active crypto policy and exposes ML-KEM-512, ML-KEM-768, and ML-KEM-1024 via `openssl list -kem-algorithms`. The scan provides the per-image and payload-level compliance verification for the base image switch campaigns.\n34\t\n35\tThis is distinct from the TLS-scanner CI owned by RHAISTRAT-7816 (which tests TLS handshake PQC negotiation): this scan verifies container crypto policy and KEM algorithm availability inside the shipped images.\n36\t\n37\t## Scope\n38\t\n39\t- Build a CI job that, for each image in the RHOAI 3.6 payload (enumerated from the RHOAI-Build-Config CSV relatedImages list), executes `update-crypto-policies --show` and `openssl list -kem-algorithms` inside the container and compares against expected values (`DEFAULT:PQ`; ML-KEM-512, ML-KEM-768, ML-KEM-1024 present).\n40\t- Produce a per-image and aggregated compliance report across the payload, consumable by the PQC readiness matrix and compliance coordination tracking.\n41\t- Encode the criterion's documented exclusions: the check applies to OpenSSL-backed components only — components using non-OpenSSL TLS stacks (rustls+ring, Java/NSS) are accounted for by OCP exception filings rather than this scan; on FIPS-mode clusters, FIPS policy deactivates PQC for this release, so `DEFAULT:PQ` non-functionality there is expected and not a compliance failure.\n42\t- Run the scan in CI on the payload build so compliance is measured continuously as images switch.\n43\t\n44\tOut of scope: TLS handshake negotiation testing (TLS-scanner CI, owned by RHAISTRAT-7816); Go ML-KEM testing (RHOAIENG-7334, RHAISTRAT-7816); remediating non-compliant images (the switch campaigns' scope).\n45\t\n46\t## Acceptance Criteria\n47\t\n48\t- The scan executes across the full RHOAI 3.6 payload and produces a per-image compliance result (policy `DEFAULT:PQ`; ML-KEM-512/768/1024 present) plus an aggregated report.\n49\t- OpenSSL-backed images that have completed the base image switch pass the scan; failures identify the image and failing check.\n50\t- Components using non-OpenSSL TLS stacks are excluded from pass/fail and cross-referenced against the exception-tracking accounting instead.\n51\t- FIPS-mode clusters are handled per the documented exclusion: PQC deactivation under FIPS policy for this release is reported as expected behavior, not a compliance failure.\n52\t- The scan runs as part of CI on the payload (not only as a one-off), so the final RHOAI 3.6 payload measurement is reproducible.\n53\t\n54\t## HLR Traceability\n55\t\n56\t- **[P0] All RHOAI runtime images must use a PQC-enabled base image... for RHOAI 3.6 / OCP 5.0** — this epic is the \"measured by automated CI scan across the RHOAI 3.6 payload\" clause of strategy AC-1.\n57\t- **[P0] Teams unable to comply by September 15 must file an OCP exception...** — the scan's exclusion accounting cross-references exception-covered components, supporting the zero-unaccounted-images outcome of AC-2.\n58\t\n59\t## Assumptions Carried Forward\n60\t\n61\t- The image list for RHOAI 3.6 follows the RHOAI-Build-Config CSV relatedImages pattern (153 relatedImages for 3.5) and the locally maintained organized inventory; the scan enumerates from the 3.6 equivalent.\n62\t- X25519MLKEM768 hybrid key exchange is backwards-compatible with non-PQC TLS 1.3 clients (IETF hybrid key exchange draft) — the scan checks algorithm availability, not client compatibility.\n63\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a0166ce5350227c5","agent_id":"a9f7d0263db104ceb","session_id":"ffc190d1-a457-4608-ad90-0fad47057ac1","uuid":"2df46733-7199-4b5d-a0e1-7dfaf77d36eb","timestamp":"2026-10-09T14:43:50.442Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8caccd4cc81c8e63","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8001 decomposition"}
