{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-852b69bccb6d0a43","type":"tool_result","content":"8:- **Languages**: Rust\n16:**Detailed**: The FMS Guardrails Orchestrator is a Rust service built on axum and tonic that provides a unified REST API for safe AI text generation. It accepts client requests on v1 and v2 API surfaces (port 8033/TCP), orchestrates content safety detection by forwarding text to configurable detector services via REST, chunks text via gRPC chunker services, and coordinates generation through a TGIS or caikit-nlp gRPC backend. A separate health server (port 8034/TCP) provides unauthenticated health and info endpoints. The service supports optional TLS and mTLS on its ingress surface, passthrough of configurable client headers to downstream services, and rewriting of X-Forwarded-Access-Token headers to Authorization Bearer for downstream authentication. Two additional v2 endpoints for OpenAI-compatible chat and text completions detection are conditionally enabled when an OpenAI-compatible generation backend is configured. The orchestrator is built for multi-architecture deployment (amd64, ppc64le, s390x) on UBI 9 minimal base images. [source: Cargo.toml:1-7, config/config.yaml:1-70, src/server/routes.rs:62-108, src/args.rs:31-47]\n26:The cryptographic stack uses rustls with the ring provider throughout (rustls, hyper-rustls, tokio-rustls, tonic TLS). Ring is not a FIPS-validated cryptographic provider, which means the service cannot claim FIPS compliance for its TLS operations. OpenSSL is present only in the build stage for protobuf compilation and is not installed in the release image. [source: Cargo.toml:33-34, 62-65, 81-83, 85-88, Dockerfile.konflux:17, 51-53]\n34:| Upstream | https://github.com/trustyai-explainability/fms-guardrails-orchestrator | auto_merge | main | -- | sync_config |\n50:| fms-guardrails-orchestr8 | Rust Service (axum + tonic) | Foundation models orchestration server |\n108:| hyper-rustls | 0.27.7 | Yes | Unknown | Cargo package dependency |\n118:| rustls | 0.23.36 | Yes | runtime-security | Cargo package dependency |\n119:| rustls-pemfile | 2.2.0 | Yes | Unknown | Cargo package dependency |\n120:| rustls-pki-types | 1.13.2 | Yes | Unknown | Cargo package dependency |\n121:| rustls-webpki | 0.103.8 | Yes | Unknown | Cargo package dependency |\n127:| tokio-rustls | 0.26.4 | Yes | Unknown | Cargo package dependency |\n187:| TLS server cert/key | kubernetes.io/tls | Rust HTTP server TLS | Platform operator / cert-manager | Unknown |\n188:| TLS client CA cert | Opaque | Rust HTTP server mTLS | Platform operator | Unknown |\n196:| /api/v1/*, /api/v2/* | POST | Header passthrough | Application-level filtering | Configured headers are forwarded to downstream services |\n198:| All endpoints | ALL | TLS / mTLS | Rust TLS acceptor | Optional server TLS and client certificate verification |\n205:| crypto-library | rustls | Rust TLS dependency is present; the cryptographic provider and FIPS mode require configuration or lockfile verification | dependency-signal |\n206:| crypto-library | tokio-rustls | Rust TLS dependency is present; the cryptographic provider and FIPS mode require configuration or lockfile verification | dependency-signal |\n207:| crypto-library | hyper-rustls | Rust TLS dependency is present; the cryptographic provider and FIPS mode require configuration or lockfile verification | dependency-signal |\n208:| crypto-provider | ring | Cargo.lock selects ring as a cryptographic provider; ring is not a FIPS-validated provider | dependency-signal |\n209:| crypto-provider | openssl | Cargo.lock selects this cryptographic provider; FIPS validation depends on build and runtime configuration | dependency-signal |\n=== PLATFORM ===\n20:Security follows a defense-in-depth posture: all shipped Go binaries are built with `CGO_ENABLED=1` and `GOEXPERIMENT=strictfipsruntime` for FIPS-compliant cryptography through system OpenSSL, operators read the cluster APIServer TLS security profile to align their TLS configuration with platform-wide policy, admission webhooks validate and default CRD resources with fail-closed policies, and per-component RBAC is scoped through dedicated ServiceAccounts and ClusterRoles. Authentication is layered across gateway-level (kube-auth-proxy OIDC, Kuadrant AuthPolicy), pod-level (kube-rbac-proxy sidecar), and application-level (Kubernetes TokenReview, SubjectAccessReview) enforcement points.\n65:**rhods-operator** is the platform root: it owns all component CRDs (DataSciencePipelines, Kueue, ModelRegistry, Ray, SparkOperator, Trainer, TrainingOperator, TrustyAI) and three service controllers (Auth, Gateway, Monitoring) that manage cross-cutting platform infrastructure. Every component operator depends on rhods-operator for activation and lifecycle management.\n71:**odh-dashboard** is the primary user interface, implementing a micro-frontend architecture with Webpack Module Federation. It watches platform CRDs (DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, KServe InferenceService, ModelRegistry, MLflow, TrustyAI, Feast) to reflect real-time platform state.\n79:| CRD-based orchestration | rhods-operator, kserve, odh-model-controller, models-as-a-service, kuberay, kueue-operator, trainer-operator, workbenches-operator, spark-operator, model-registry-operator, feast-module-operator, mlflow-operator, ai-gateway-operator, ogx-k8s-operator, trustyai-service-operator, data-science-pipelines-operator, mcp-lifecycle-operator, mcp-lifecycle-module-operator, llm-d-batch-gateway-operator | Each component defines cluster-scoped CRDs watched by the platform operator; component operators reconcile their own CRDs into namespace-scoped workloads |\n94:| redhat-ods-applications / opendatahub | Platform applications | odh-dashboard, odh-model-controller, kserve controllers, models-as-a-service, model-registry-operator, notebooks, data-science-pipelines-operator, training-operator, trustyai-service-operator, kueue-operator, ai-gateway-operator |\n106:| OpenShift Routes (various namespaces) | rhods-operator, model-registry-operator, odh-observability, trustyai-service-operator | Route resources per component | OpenShift HAProxy router | TLS termination at router; kube-rbac-proxy at pod level | odh-dashboard, model-registry REST, Prometheus proxies, TrustyAI service | observed |\n164:| FIPS-compliant build (strictfipsruntime) | rhods-operator, kserve (kserve-module), kube-auth-proxy, models-as-a-service, odh-model-controller, odh-dashboard, model-registry-operator, ai-gateway-operator, trainer-operator, workbenches-operator, feast-module-operator, mlflow-operator, spark-operator, kueue-operator, kuberay, mcp-lifecycle-module-operator, mcp-lifecycle-operator, ogx-k8s-operator, trustyai-service-operator, data-science-pipelines-operator | CGO_ENABLED=1 + GOEXPERIMENT=strictfipsruntime routes Go crypto through system OpenSSL |\n165:| UBI 9 base images | All shipped Go components | Red Hat Universal Base Image 9 provides FIPS-validated OpenSSL and security updates |\n189:| trustyai-service-operator | 0 | 0 | 1 | Fail |\n279:The platform comprises 72 analyzed components including: 19+ operators/controllers (rhods-operator, kserve x4, odh-model-controller, odh-dashboard-operator, models-as-a-service, model-registry-operator, ai-gateway-operator, trainer-operator, workbenches-operator, feast-module-operator, mlflow-operator, data-science-pipelines-operator, kuberay, kueue-operator, spark-operator, training-operator, trustyai-service-operator, ogx-k8s-operator, mcp-lifecycle-operator, mcp-lifecycle-module-operator, llm-d-batch-gateway-operator), 145 CRDs, 210 admission webhooks, 122 services, and 1658 RBAC entries across all components.\n338:- **FIPS Compliance**: All shipped Go binaries built with strictfipsruntime; Python components inherit FIPS from UBI base OpenSSL\n356:**Supply chain consistency.** All analyzed Go components use the FIPS-compliant build pattern (CGO_ENABLED=1 + GOEXPERIMENT=strictfipsruntime) on UBI 9 base images, providing strong build-time FIPS posture. However, the upstream KServe core manager Dockerfile uses CGO_ENABLED=0 (static linking, not FIPS-compliant), relying on the Konflux build pipeline to override these flags for downstream builds. Python components (KServe model servers, data-science-pipelines) inherit FIPS from the UBI base OpenSSL but have no explicit FIPS opt-in in their application code.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a0166ce5350227c5","agent_id":"a9f7d0263db104ceb","session_id":"ffc190d1-a457-4608-ad90-0fad47057ac1","uuid":"28104ff6-0f35-46c1-8f9e-b9fc72b7a171","timestamp":"2026-10-09T14:45:31.581Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-852b69bccb6d0a43","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8001 decomposition"}
