erred HLR set is entirely P0 under a hard deadline, so the all-P0 epic set is correct, not a priority collapse (Health Warning 5 documents this). Priority inheritance holds — E001, which gates the P0 campaigns E003/E004, is P0. No P0 HLR is unmapped; no orphaned requirements.\n\n### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n\nCross-check of the summary diagram against epic frontmatter: exact match. The diagram's four edges (E001→E003, E001→E004, E005→E006, E007→E008) correspond one-to-one with the only non-empty `dependencies` lists (E003: [E001], E004: [E001], E006: [E005], E008: [E007]); all other epics have empty dependencies, matching the seven declared roots. Arrow direction follows the dependency→dependent convention. No circular dependencies, no edges to nonexistent epics, no main-plan dependency on a branch epic (no branch files exist).\n\nEvery blocking edge is a justified Rule 3 gate grounded in the strategy: the AIPCC RHEL 9.8 rebase date gates rhel9-ELS adoption (strategy Dependencies table), PQC python/nodejs variant availability gates the E004 family's existence (RHOAIENG-7195), the guardrails spike determines the migration approach (RHOAIENG-7452), and the trainer TLS audit determines whether remediation work exists at all (RHOAIENG-7196). All four are true gates with complete `gated_by` + `gate_failure_impact` (rewrite/rewrite/remove/remove).\n\nNo unjustified serialization: E002 correctly has no edge (target base confirmed available), and the campaigns↔E006/E008, campaigns→E010, campaigns→E011, E009↔investigations, and E010↔E011 non-edges are each justified with correct rule citations. In particular the decomposition correctly treats the CI scan's and performance measurement's consumption of built payload images as verification-time configuration references (Rule 13) enforced as acceptance criteria (\"verified by the automated payload CI scan\"), not DAG edges — exactly the AC-gate pattern the rules require for image-digest-style coupling. No `konflux-onboarding`, `license-validation`, or `repo-onboarding` epics exist, so the mandated-parallel pairings (rules 7, 8, 10) are not applicable and are not violated. The AIPCC third-party dependency is correctly modeled per Rule 21 as a precondition with tiered delivery (Investigation to confirm, not an RHOAI implementation epic), with the Tier 1/Tier 2 split documented. The choice of `gate_failure_impact` over `-BRANCH-` files is sound and documented: each bounded unknown's negative outcome produces no in-scope epics (exception filing is each team's own out-of-scope work), so there is no per-outcome epic set to branch on. Critical path length 2 is within the S-strategy heuristic (1-2). Scanning epic content for uncaptured data dependencies found none beyond those explicitly adjudicated in the DAG justification table (readiness-matrix sequencing is handled as an AC plus stated sequencing in E001/E007/E009, a coordination record rather than a build-against artifact).\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n\nComponent/team tuples are all distinct and correctly separated: three family campaigns (AI Core Platform / Notebooks Images / Model Runtimes, each DevOps/Build), the AIPCC investigation, the guardrails and trainer Investigation+Implementation pairs, coordination (DevOps), CI scan (CI/CD), and performance validation (PerfScale). All component values are canonical names from `.context/rhai-components.txt`, and the non-obvious campaign mappings are logged in the summary as required. The family campaigns spanning many component teams' images is the strategy's own structure (each team executes its switch via linked tracking tickets; the epic coordinates the campaign) and qualifies as the same logical change per boundary rule 2 — one named target base per campaign. Grouping the four python/nodejs base rows into one E004 campaign is defensible: they share a single gating dependency (RHOAIENG-7195), one coordination owner, and one change pattern.\n\nNo scope duplication: the readiness matrix is built only by E009, the scan only by E010, the campaigns partition the image payload by family, and the TLS remediations (E006/E008) are disjoint from the base swaps (different work surfaces on the same components, correctly parallel per rules 14/15). No work-product type mixing — E011's threshold definition is an AC on a benchmarking Implementation, not bundled content authoring. No epic is plausibly multi-sprint for its own scope (campaign execution is distributed to owning teams via tracking tickets).\n\nMinor: the strategy's weekly-chase mitigation is scoped in two places — each campaign's \"chase unlinked tickets weekly\" and E009's \"run the weekly reminder cadence and escalate\" — all under DevOps/Build. The per-family vs. aggregate split is defensible, but as written two actors are assigned the same chasing activity for the same teams.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n\nAll three Investigations are genuine: E001's outcome changes E004's existence (remove) and E003's scope (rewrite); E005's outcome rewrites E006's approach; E007's outcome determines whether E008 exists at all (remove). Each has downstream epics that depend on its outcome. All eight Implementations produce concrete artifacts (switched images and pipeline updates, migrated TLS stacks, readiness matrix, CI scan job, threshold/methodology/benchmark report).\n\nGating metadata is complete and correct: every epic with an Investigation in `dependencies` has `gated_by` set to that Investigation; every `gated_by` target appears in the epic's direct `dependencies` list; every `gate_failure_impact` has both `action` and a substantive `fallback_approach` describing what actually changes on failure. No scheduling-only gates masquerading as outcome gates. E011 is correctly an Implementation: the unresolved performance threshold is a parameter (domain input) that does not change which epics exist, handled as an AC with PM/perf-lead approval — matching the decomposition rules' two-way distinction, and documented in Health Warning 1. E001 correctly combines the variant-availability and rebase-date unknowns: both are resolved by the same AIPCC engagement under RHOAIENG-7195 and produce one deliverable.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n\nType-appropriate signal sets are present in all 11 epics — the three Investigations carry `investigation_signals` only, the eight Implementations carry `ai_signals` only, with no cross-contamination — and all 11 `-ai-signals.md` rationale files exist with the correct rubric per type. Frontmatter values match the rationale files in every case.\n\nSignal values are consistent with the rubric conditions and the strategy content: E001's `source_accessibility: 0` and `human_judgment_required: -2` correctly capture that the gating answer is a pending external team's commitment (the rubric's canonical 0 case); E002's `open_questions: +1` correctly reflects the confirmed-available target base, in contrast to E003/E004's `-1` for their gated unknowns; E004's `external_dependency: -1` is right (the variants it consumes do not exist yet and are delivered by another team), while E003's 0 is a defensible distinction (image published; only internal timing uncertain); E007's `source_accessibility: +1` is honestly reasoned against the trainer architecture context (Go operator + Python initializers, Rust posture unconfirmed — the readable artifacts determine the answer either way); E011's `open_questions: -1` and `human_process_gates: -1` match the strategy's NEEDS DOMAIN INPUT flag and PM/perf-lead approval requirement. No signal value contradicts the rubric.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n\nEvery epic has testable acceptance criteria derived from the strategy: enumerated per-family switched-or-exceptioned outcomes with zero-unaccounted accounting, the exact AC-1 verification commands (`update-crypto-policies --show` → `DEFAULT:PQ`; `openssl list -kem-algorithms` → ML-KEM-512/768/1024), builder-stage-unchanged checks, no-HA-change checks, gate conditions as explicit ACs on the gated campaigns, evidence-based audit outcomes, and deadline-bound deliverables. Rule-mandated ACs are present wherever applicable: all five replacement-style epics (E002/E003/E004 base swaps, E006/E008 TLS migrations) carry rollback ACs; the campaigns carry build-pipeline-green ACs; the FIPS-mode exclusion from AC-1 is encoded as an expected-behavior AC on E010 rather than a compliance failure. No `docs-authoring` epic exists, correctly: the strategy's Prerequisites list Documentation Support as \"Pending human review\", not \"Yes\", so Rule 11 is not triggered — and Health Warning 3 documents the revision path if human review confirms support is required. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID (verified by scan); cross-epic couplings are described in plain terms (\"the automated payload CI scan\", \"the platform-wide switch campaign\").\n\nMinor: E009's AC requiring the matrix to be \"created before the first outcome publication arrives\" depends on the unenforced relative timing of parallel root epics — the AIPCC confirmation or trainer audit could complete before the matrix exists, failing the AC as written even though no work is harmed (the outcome would simply be recorded at matrix creation). The AC should tolerate that ordering.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n\nThe combined epic set accounts for the strategy's full scope: all four base-image mapping rows (three switch campaigns plus the TrustyAI openjdk row's exception path, tracked through E009's matrix and zero-unaccounted accounting per Health Warning 6); all three likely-exception components (guardrails via E005/E006, trainer data cache via E007/E008, TrustyAI via E009); all three impacted teams plus the Performance team prerequisite; the strategy's \"to be created\" PQC Readiness Matrix design doc (E009); AC-1's automated-CI-scan measurement clause (E010, with the scope trap it implies explicitly provisioned); AC-2's zero-unaccounted outcome (E009 plus the campaigns); AC-3's ticket linking (E009); and the Performance NFR's missing threshold (E011). All four NFRs are carried into epic ACs (Security → the TLS migrations; Backwards compatibility → opportunistic-hybrid ACs; Availability → no-HA-change ACs; Performance → E011). Strategy-level context is not silently dropped: risks, assumptions, and open questions appear in dedicated carried-forward sections on the relevant epics, the Mara/Devin escalation decision is carried into E009, and every epic's out-of-scope list consistently respects the strategy's exclusions (RHAISTRAT-7816 scope, per-team exception filing, ML-DSA, post-OCP-5.0 evolution, FIPS+PQC). The pending process gates are recorded as unfavorable human-process signals and Health Warning 2 rather than ignored.\n\nMinor: the strategy's base image mapping table sums to roughly 114 images (~68 + ~34 + 5 + 4 + 1 + 1 + 1) against the 153 relatedImages cited from the RHOAI 3.5 CSV. The residual ~39 images have no explicit family assignment in any epic; their coverage relies implicitly on E009's every-image matrix and zero-unaccounted AC. The campaigns' enumeration from the CSV and organized inventory likely absorbs them, but the gap is neither reconciled nor flagged.\n"}}],"model":"zai-org/GLM-5.3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":90053,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a0166ce5350227c5","agent_id":"a9f7d0263db104ceb","session_id":"ffc190d1-a457-4608-ad90-0fad47057ac1","uuid":"1b2d94a7-2376-4a17-b069-ac97cd04b29c","timestamp":"2026-10-09T14:52:31.281Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8001 decomposition"}
